<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Posidonius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 135–c. 51 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Posidonius of Rhodes, Posidonius of Apamea</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Posidonius was a Greek Stoic philosopher, historian and scientist from Apamea in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> who settled on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>, where he ran a famous school and held public office. He wrote on philosophy, history, geography, astronomy and the tides, and estimated the circumference of the Earth. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> visited him, and his works, now lost, influenced many later writers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/posidonius.jpg" alt="Posidonius" width="320" height="586" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Posidonio,_replica_augustea_(23_ac.-14_dc_ca)_da_originale_del_100-50_ac._ca._6142.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
